{{smaller block|“A ''trefle'',” quath he, “trewlie! his treuth is full litell!”
{{right|''Piers Plowman's Crede'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 352.}}}}

{{smaller block|This ydelnesse is the thurrok of alle wikked and vileyns thoghtes, and of alle jangles, ''trufles'', and of alle ordure.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Parson's Tale.}}}}

3. An idle speech or tale; vain or foolish talk; twaddle; nonsense; absurdity.

{{smaller block|Holde thi tonge, Mercy!
It is but a ''trufle'' that thow tellest.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xviii. 147.}}}}

4. Anything of slight value or moment; a paltry matter; an insignificant fact, circumstance, object, amount, etc.: often used in the adverbial phrase ''a trifle:'' as, to feel ''a trifle'' annoyed.

{{smaller block|Thus ther stondes in stale the stif kyng hisseluen,
Talkkande bifore the hyȝe table of ''trifles'' ful hende.
{{right|''Sir Gawayne and the Green Knight'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 108.}}}}

{{smaller block|A snapper-up of unconsidered ''trifles''.
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., iv. 3. 26.}}}}

{{smaller block|The bank itself was small and grave, and ''a trifle'' dingy.
{{right|''C. Reade'', Love me Little, xi.}}}}

5. A dish or confection consisting mainly of whipped cream or some light substitute, as the beaten whites of eggs, and usually containing fruit or almonds, and cake or pastry soaked in wine or brandy.

readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|I really must confess that the Log, for long, long after I first went to sea, . . . could be compared to nothing more fitly than a dish of ''trifle'', anciently called syllabub, with a stray plum here and there scattered at the bottom.
{{right|''M. Scott'', Tom Cringle's Log, i.}}}}

6. Common pewter, such as is used for ordinary utensils, composed of eighty parts of tin and twenty of lead.

'''trifle'''<sup>1</sup> (trī′fl), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''trifled'', ppr. ''trifling''. [< ME. ''triflen'', ''trifelen'', ''tryflen'', ''treoflen'', ''troflen'', ''truflen'', < OF. ''truffler'', ''truffer'', jest, mock: see ''trifle''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] I. ''intrans.'' 1. To jest; make sport; hence, to use mockery; treat something with derision, flippancy, or a lack of proper respect: often followed by ''with''.

{{smaller block|The stede [a church] is holy, and is y-zet to bidde god, naȝt uor to iangli, uor to lheȝȝe [laugh], ne uorto ''trufly''.
{{right|''Ayenbite of Inwyt'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 214.}}}}

{{smaller block|Look to yourself, dear sir,
And ''trifle'' not ''with'' danger that attends you.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Double Marriage, iv. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|For is there nothing to ''trifle with'' but God and his Service?
{{right|''Stillingfleet'', Sermons, I. ii.}}}}

2†. To use trickery or deception; cheat; lie.

{{smaller block|Thow art feble and false, and noghte bot faire wordes; . . .
I red thowe trette of a trewe, and ''trofle'' no lengere.
{{right|''Morte Arthure'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2933.}}}}

3. To talk or act idly; busy one's self with trivial or useless things; act frivolously; waste one's time; dally; idle.

{{smaller block|''Treoflinge'' heo smot her & ther in another tale sone.
{{right|''Rob. of Gloucester'' (ed. Morris and Skeat, II. 21).}}}}

{{smaller block|We would not ''trifle'' long at this place.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', II. ii. 28.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|I can only ''trifle'' in this Review. It takes me some time to think about serious subjects.
{{right|''Sydney Smith'', To Francis Jeffrey, July, 1810.}}}}

'''4.''' To play, as by lightly handling or touching something; toy.

{{smaller block|Hold still thy hands, moue not thy feete, beware thou of ''tryfling''. {{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 75.}}}}

{{smaller block|Stretch your blind hands and ''trifle'' with a match
Over a mine of Greek fire. {{right|''Browning'', An Epistle.}}}}

{{smaller block|The two gentlemen had finished supper, and were now ''trifling'' with cigars and maraschino.
{{right|''R. L. Stevenson'', The Dynamiter, p. 134.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To turn into jest or sport; hence, to treat lightly or flippantly; play with.

{{smaller block|How dothe oure bysshop ''tryfle'' and mocke vs, sythe he kepeth aboute hym the greatest brybour and robbor in all Fraunce, and wolde that we shulde gyue hym oure money. {{right|''Berners'', tr. of Froissart's Chron., I. cc.}}}}

'''2.''' To spend on trifles; pass idly or foolishly; waste; fritter: often followed by ''away''.

{{smaller block|We ''trifle'' time in words. {{right|''Ford'', Broken Heart, v. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|The scarcest of all [medals] is a Pescennius Niger on a medallion well preserved. It was coined at Antioch, where this emperor ''trifled away'' his time till he lost his life and empire.
{{right|''Addison'', Remarks on Italy (Works, ed. Bohn, I. 504).}}}}

'''3.''' To utter or perform lightly or carelessly.

{{smaller block|She used him for her sport, like what he was, to ''trifle'' a leisure sentence or two with. {{right|''Lamb'', Old Actors.}}}}

'''4.''' To reduce to a trifle; make trivial or of no importance. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|This sore night
Hath ''trifled'' former knowings.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, ii. 4. 4.}}}}

