'''trifle-ring''' (trī′fl-ring), ''n.'' A ring having some hidden mechanism or play of parts, as a gimmel-ring, puzzle-ring, or one composed of three or more hoops working on pivots.

'''trifling''' (trī′fling), ''n.'' [< ME. *''trifling'', *''trufling'', ''trouflyng''; verbal n. of ''trifle'', ''v.''] The act or conduct of one who trifles, in any sense.

{{smaller block|He returned his answer by a letter dated at Crogh the thirtith of October, 1579, vsing therein nothing but ''triflings'' and delaies. {{right|''Stanihurst'', Chron. of Ireland, an. 1579 (Holinshed's [Chron., I.).}}

Presumptuous dallyings, or impertinent ''triflings'' with God. {{right|''Barrow'', Sermons, I. xxxi.}}}}

'''trifling''' (trī′fling), ''p. a.'' [Ppr. of ''trifle'', ''v.''] 1. Inclined to trifle; lacking depth or earnestness; shallow; frivolous; idle; vain.

{{smaller block|His serious impassioned look . . . was so completely sincere and true that her ''trifling'' nature was impressed in spite of everything. {{right|''Mrs. Oliphant'', Poor Gentleman, xxxvi.}}}}

2. Trivial; unimportant; insignificant; slight; small.

{{smaller block|My Arab insisted to attend me thither, and, upon his arrival, I made some ''trifling'' presents, and then took my leave. {{right|''Bruce'', Source of the Nile, I. 54.}}}}

